// A sine wave evaluated in closed form at the solver's own time:
// y = offset + amplitude * sin(2*pi*f*t + phase).
//
// This is the `ct` twin `se.sig.src` spent its whole life without. The
// difference is one line — `sim.time()` instead of a state — and it is worth a
// package of its own because of what it does to accuracy.
//
// `src.Sine` is DISCRETE: its output is zero-order held across a base step, so
// a plant integrating it sees a staircase, and RK4 collapses to first order
// across that path no matter how good the integrator is. This node declares no
// `rate` and holds no state, so §10.5 re-evaluates its `output()` at every
// minor step and `sim.time()` returns the instant the solver is actually at.
// Feeding an RK4 plant from here keeps RK4's order.
//
// ── WHAT IT COSTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `src.Sine` accumulates NORMALISED PHASE and wraps it, for reasons its own
// header sets out: the increment carries no 2*pi, wrapping is one exact floor
// subtraction, and precision does not decay because the accumulator never
// holds more than one turn. `sin(2*pi*f*t)` has none of that. As `t` grows the
// argument eats mantissa bits, so a long run drifts out of phase with its own
// reference — at 1 kHz and an hour of sim time the argument is past 2e7 rad
// and roughly nine significant digits of the phase survive.
//
// That is not a defect to be fixed here; it is the price of being continuous.
// A wrapped accumulator can only advance at sample instants, and a value that
// only moves at sample instants is exactly what "discrete" means. Nor can this
// node offer `src.Sine`'s mid-cycle start through the IC channel (§13.4) — it
// has no state to override. Shift it with `phase`.
//
// Reach for this one when the sine is part of the plant: a forcing function, a
// disturbance, a shaker input. Reach for `se.sig.src.Sine` when it is part of
// the signal processing, when the run is long, or when a sampled source is
// what you actually mean.
package se.sig.ct;

node Sine {
    units { U; }

    settings {
        amplitude: double(U)   = 1.0;
        f:         double(Hz)  = 1.0;
        phase:     double(rad) = 0.0;  // radians is the only declarable
                                       // angle unit (§4.5)
        offset: double(U) = 0.0;
    }

    outputs { y: double(U); }

    // No `rate` and no state: that pair is what puts this node in the
    // minor-step output pass (§10.5). Adding either would silently make it
    // zero-order held again and undo the point of the node.
    vars { w: double(rad/s); }

    // The 2*pi lives here, on erased units, for `src.Sine`'s reason: once angle
    // is a base dimension, `(Hz)` and `(rad/s)` are genuinely incompatible and
    // `2*pi*f` cannot be written as a derived setting. It is honest arithmetic
    // on erased units (§4.4), so it belongs in a body.
    //
    // There is no Nyquist warning to give, unlike every source in `se.sig.src`.
    // This node has no sample rate to alias against; what limits it is the
    // solver's step, and that is the model's business rather than the node's.
    init() { var.w = 6.283185307179586 * param.f; }

    output() {
        out.y = param.offset + param.amplitude * std::sin(var.w * sim.time() + param.phase);
    }
}
